WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.710
My guest this week is

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:04.590
the award winning Nigerian
writer and author,

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.200
Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.970
Named by Time Magazine as one of its

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:10.890
most influential people.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:14.850
Her writing covers everything
from war to feminism,

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:18.030
to most recently, a very
personal take on grief,

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:21.480
having lost both of her
parents in quick succession.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.120
I want to ask her about being described

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.090
as fearless, for speaking her mind,

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:29.010
and also what it was like to team up

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:32.013
with Beyonce to try and promote feminism.

00:00:46.494 --> 00:00:47.327
Chimamanda welcome.

00:00:47.327 --> 00:00:50.040
It's so lovely to have you
with me on this program,

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.009
and see you in person.

00:00:52.009 --> 00:00:52.842
I know.

00:00:52.842 --> 00:00:53.675
Thank you Emma,

00:00:53.675 --> 00:00:54.508
for having me.

00:00:54.508 --> 00:00:55.530
It's lovely to see you.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.620
Well, the last time we
spoke, was last year.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.740
And, you had just penned
a short book called

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.549
Notes on Grief.

00:01:03.549 --> 00:01:06.450
And, we were talking in
a difficult time for you,

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:07.560
but you wanted to talk

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.340
about losing your father
and then losing your mother.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.900
And, you said to me you were
trying to understand grief

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:17.520
and process it.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:18.353
And, I wonder,

00:01:18.353 --> 00:01:20.550
I wanted to start by
asking how you are today.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:24.030
Well, I think I'm learning that a lot

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:27.420
of living is sort of
pretending to be fine.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.183
So, I think I'm doing that very well.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.540
Yeah, I'm fine, I'm fine.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:34.410
Thank you for asking.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:39.410
But, I think everything
happy that happens to me now

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.200
is always tainted by a kind of,

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.260
sort of like a residual sadness.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:48.180
And, it's because I just adored my parents

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:51.861
and it still feels very,
very disorienting for me.

00:01:51.861 --> 00:01:52.890
I bet

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:55.170
I mean, you talked about
those stages as well,

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:58.020
you know, coming undone, feeling unmoored,

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.750
but, you are right about
the world expects you

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.030
to get back to some place.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:05.370
And, you have your
responsibilities as well.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.560
And, you do, you just sort of,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.600
but, you carry something with you always.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.850
And, I, it's interesting because I thought

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:15.060
that I would be more aware
of this kind of emptiness

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.250
in my life when I'm sad.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.003
But, actually it's when I'm
happy, it's when I'm having

00:02:20.003 --> 00:02:22.410
a lovely day and suddenly I think, my God,

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.450
I can't FaceTime daddy.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.750
And, I had thing in the US last month

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:31.080
where I got a lovely medal,
and I love those things,

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.880
cause I just love shiny things,

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.000
and I really did want to take my phone

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.110
and FaceTime my parents,

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:39.300
because, that's what
I've done my whole life

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:41.970
when I've been given something meaningful.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.530
You know, it's to call my parents.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.957
Well, they're the people who cheer you up.

00:02:45.957 --> 00:02:48.304
So, I really, I mean,

00:02:48.304 --> 00:02:50.155
I really was going to FaceTime them

00:02:50.155 --> 00:02:52.020
and thought, wait, I can't.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:56.640
And, so, it's those moments
that are really just painful.

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.470
Something else you said,
which has stayed with me,

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:01.410
is the idea that we live in this world,

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:03.847
and we know death's a part of it,

00:03:03.847 --> 00:03:06.810
and we're still so surprised.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.400
It's incredible.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.130
And, even when we see, as I do now,

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:14.550
life is short, you really need
to think about what matters

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:15.843
and how you want to live.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.950
It's also at the same time a
strange thing to live life,

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.350
always thinking about death,

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:26.490
because, in a way, death is
sort of, it's the unlife,

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:27.933
it's the not life.

00:03:28.767 --> 00:03:30.994
And, so, it's a strange thing I think.

00:03:30.994 --> 00:03:33.090
I think a lot changed for me

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:34.203
when my parents died,

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.850
in the way that I think about
life and how I want to live.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.050
But, I think death is
still surprising, too.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.140
It still is.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.280
One of the ways,

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:45.810
I dunno how much this speaks to you,

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.550
but, people talk about faith,

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:50.310
and that helping them
through these sorts of times.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:51.450
Do you have that in your life?

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:53.760
Have you come back to that?

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.220
It's very complicated.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:58.860
After my father died, I
started, I was raised Catholic.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.770
Yes, I'm Catholic, well, raised Catholic.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:02.940
There's a difference I think for me,

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:06.180
between being Catholic
and being raised Catholic.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.280
One doesn't have a choice and one,

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.560
the other, you kind of have a choice.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.560
And, I started going to mass,
because, I found it soothing,

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.810
but, particularly, because
it was what my father did.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:23.040
My father was just this
lovely faithful Catholic.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.643
But, then I had trouble with
the Catholic Church in Nigeria,

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.503
and I was so angry, that I stopped going.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.550
And, it's a long story, but,
I'll just say very briefly,

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:38.400
that when my mother died,
at my mother's funeral,

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:43.400
a priest used the opportunity
of my mother's funeral

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.450
to attack me about an
interview I had given.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.490
And, so, imagine just sitting there

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:52.800
just being completely lost,

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.860
because, you still do not
understand what happened.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.590
Your mother's gone, and my
mother died so suddenly,

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:02.190
and a priest at her funeral
takes the microphone

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:05.370
and says, Chimamanda
was attacking the church

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:07.980
because she says the church
is too obsessed with money,

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.530
which they are, which I said,

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.990
but, at my mother's funeral, you know.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:14.520
So, that made me so angry.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:17.670
And, I haven't been back since.

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.260
Did you say anything at the time?

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:20.093
Or were you...

00:05:20.093 --> 00:05:20.970
Oh, I did.

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:22.818
I got up and I said,

00:05:22.818 --> 00:05:26.643
I need to speak because,
you cannot do this.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:32.490
And, I then I did spend a
lot of time seeking redress,

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:35.073
writing to bishops and, nothing.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:38.670
I think how religious institutions

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:41.670
use their power over individuals,

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:44.520
but, also how it intersects with politics,

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.490
is in people's minds,
especially in America,

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.560
at the moment, since Roe v Wade,

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:53.070
with the overturning of that ruling.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.470
And, I wanted to ask you about it because,

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:57.690
we see in Christianity with Christianity,

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:01.350
it being used by
politicians, by legislators,

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:04.110
to defend the rowing back

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.300
of those particular rights for women.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.970
And, you of course are very
well known for speaking out

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.880
about feminism and the rights of women.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:13.380
You know, someone raised Catholic,

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:16.833
how do you look at that,
and that situation?

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:19.260
Well, the first thing I think

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:23.162
is that one should never
impose one's religious views

00:06:23.162 --> 00:06:25.620
on anyone in democracy.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:30.620
And, I've often felt quite
respectful of those people

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:36.540
who for religious reasons do not support,

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:38.280
or want to have abortions.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:40.890
I think that's fantastic for them.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:41.723
Right?

00:06:41.723 --> 00:06:44.250
But, to somehow insist that
a woman doesn't have a right

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:45.510
to do what she wants with her body,

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:48.600
and, to then justify that using religion,

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.150
I think it's just completely
unacceptable for democracy.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:53.580
Just, it makes no sense to me.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:54.510
I mean, for me, being Catholic

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:56.250
doesn't mean it's sort of
agreeing with everything.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:57.750
Even when I was a child growing up,

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:00.570
I was a bit of a, I think
at Catholicism classes,

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:02.220
I think that they were irritated with me

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.470
because, I would sort of poke holes

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:08.070
at some of the things
that we were being taught.

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:10.620
If collectively as a society

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.050
we really believe that women are equal,

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.300
we would not be having this conversation.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:16.860
If you step back and think about it,

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:21.090
it's actually almost surreal to say,

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:22.833
your body doesn't belong to you,

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.100
is a very strange thing, I think.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:27.270
A lot of people describe you

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:28.800
as fearless now.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.860
in this age that we live in,
where social media was meant

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:34.199
to liberate us and allow us to share.

00:07:34.199 --> 00:07:36.390
But, in fact has imprisoned us, I think.

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:38.520
And, there's a real
concern, in particular,

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.250
that women are being pushed
out of the public square,

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:43.740
not saying what they really think.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:45.390
Do you have that concern?

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:46.290
For myself, no.

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:47.343
For women, yes.

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:53.400
You know, when I'm called
fearless, I guess it's nice,

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:58.353
but, I don't really think
of myself as fearless.

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:01.260
This is what I've always been, really.

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:03.480
I think part of the reason
I'm so fiercely feminist

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:04.740
about the way I look at the world,

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:07.050
is that, I knew from a very young age

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:10.710
that to be a girl and to be as I was,

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:14.100
which was sort of
outspoken, asking questions,

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:16.050
was kind of seen as odd.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:17.810
So, even now, I think,

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:21.060
as sort of an ostensibly full-grown woman,

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:24.390
to be told, you really say what you think.

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:27.540
It's a thing that's a compliment
for women, not for men.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:29.910
But, I think we're also living in times

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:31.803
where to say what you think...

00:08:31.803 --> 00:08:33.115
Yes, that's true.

00:08:33.115 --> 00:08:34.770
...has also become something

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:36.630
that people are called fearless for.

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:37.463
Yes, yes, that's true.

00:08:37.463 --> 00:08:38.760
Which again, to use
your word from earlier,

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.653
has a surrealism to it.

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.940
I know it's only one platform,
but it is the platform

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:47.280
a lot of the time of debate
in terms of social media

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:52.020
with Twitter now being run
by Elon Musk, some people,

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.600
men and women, have left
the platform in process.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.070
Equally, others are saying,

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:57.900
now there will be, or could be,

00:08:57.900 --> 00:08:59.820
real freedom of speech.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:01.830
Do you have a view on that?

00:09:01.830 --> 00:09:03.900
I don't think one person

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:05.580
should be in control of a platform

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:10.580
that has that kind of
outsized social influence.

00:09:12.108 --> 00:09:13.860
I just don't think it makes sense.

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:14.693
And, you know, people can say,

00:09:14.693 --> 00:09:15.840
Oh, well he bought it, it's his,

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:17.280
or before they would say,

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:20.010
Oh, they're just really beholden
to only their shareholders.

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:22.710
But, I think it's a public utility.

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:24.510
We should think of it as a public utility.

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:26.490
So, it really shouldn't be one person.

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:29.220
This is the way we live now, sadly.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:31.113
And, we have to figure out a way to,

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:37.440
I'm going to sound like a a
preacher, we need to do better.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.150
But, I really think we do.

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:41.580
I don't think we can change,

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:44.160
I think we can change
how we use the platforms.

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:48.060
I really detest this kind of piling on

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:49.620
that happens when people
have said something

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:51.540
that somebody doesn't like.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:54.630
And, you know, it's not just
that you criticize them,

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:57.840
it's that you publicize their address,

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:01.113
you put their children's schools
online, that sort of thing.

00:10:01.113 --> 00:10:03.120
And, you try to get
them to lose their jobs.

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.790
I have just never felt
that that is in any way

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:09.120
a reasonable response to bad speech.

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:09.960
Right?

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.832
But, I also sometimes
wonder why there isn't more

00:10:12.832 --> 00:10:17.373
that's done to those
who pile on on people.

00:10:18.598 --> 00:10:21.153
So, so often we'll say,
Oh, that's terrible.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:22.823
But, that's it.

00:10:22.823 --> 00:10:23.656
I mean, I'm not on Twitter,

00:10:23.656 --> 00:10:26.160
but, I think if I were, and
there's a reason I'm not,

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:27.570
it just would not go well,

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:28.770
because, I think I would go after

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:30.510
the people who pile on people and be like,

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:34.560
you're just a piece of,
and you need to stop.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.810
I mean, you have had people,

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:38.340
even though you're not on that platform,

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.179
be very upset about various things...

00:10:40.179 --> 00:10:41.220
Oh, yeah.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:42.053
...that you've said.

00:10:42.053 --> 00:10:43.893
And, has that affected you?

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:49.380
Yes and no.

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:52.050
I think, so in general, I think this idea

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:54.390
that we're supposed to
say that we're fine,

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:57.810
when you hear that the tons

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:01.140
of people maligning you, publicly,

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:03.180
to say that I'm fine would be to lie.

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:04.860
And, I think it's either you're lying

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:05.910
or you're a narcissist.

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:09.150
Because, how can you
not be affected by it?

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:12.270
And, so, the first time that
I heard oh, they're saying

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.120
that you are terrible, and
that you're a murderer,

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.040
I was just deeply hurt.

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:18.780
I was deeply, deeply hurt.

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:19.613
What was that after?

00:11:19.613 --> 00:11:20.880
Was that after something you'd said, or?

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:21.780
Yes.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:22.613
Yeah.

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:25.050
Well, surely, I mean,
I didn't kill anyone.

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:28.440
Yeah, I know, it's good
to check, in interviews.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:29.610
There are some people
I would like to kill.

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:31.221
No, I'm kidding, I'm kidding.

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:37.203
So, it was very hurtful,
but, it won't make me change.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:41.643
It will not make me
change how I live my life.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:44.730
It hasn't and it won't.

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:46.230
I'm still going to say what I think.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:48.813
So, you don't, you haven't self censored,

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:51.900
because, that's the real
risk here, isn't it?

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:54.487
When we get into this position.

00:11:54.487 --> 00:11:57.600
Because, I did notice you have not wanted

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.670
to answer questions about

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:01.380
when you said in an
interview, for instance,

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:03.057
trans women are women,

00:12:03.057 --> 00:12:05.490
and not necessarily to go
into that issue per se,

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:08.850
but, I wonder why you haven't
wanted to talk about that.

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:12.600
No, what I haven't
wanted to do is say more,

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:15.200
but, I've said what I said,
and that's what I think.

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:17.310
And, why not say more?

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:18.480
Because of the reaction?

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:19.392
Or you said...

00:12:19.392 --> 00:12:20.429
Well, because it's been
said, I just think...

00:12:20.429 --> 00:12:21.262
Complete?

00:12:21.262 --> 00:12:22.095
Yeah.

00:12:22.095 --> 00:12:24.413
Because I just think also that
this is sort of this age of,

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:28.410
like a friend of mine said,

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:29.370
you said what you said,

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.710
anybody asking you more about this

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:34.620
is just trying to stoke outrage

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.510
and make it more of a thing.

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:39.450
And, which was never my intention, right.

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:43.200
I said what I said, it's what I believe,

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.570
it's what I still believe.

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:48.240
But, then having people say to me,

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:49.350
tell me more, tell me more.

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:50.670
I'm just like, no.

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:53.040
And, also, you know, I have a full life.

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:55.350
There're many, many
things I'm interested in.

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:57.371
And, yeah.

00:12:57.371 --> 00:12:59.610
And, I suppose, when people have thought

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:02.580
about that particular
issue and gender and sex.

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:05.940
There's a lot of talk
now about J. K. Rowling

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:10.050
and how she's continued to share views

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:12.840
and be very alive on
platforms, such as Twitter,

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:14.310
to this particular topic.

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:15.690
But, there's now a bit more

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:18.450
of people in the public
eye who've worked with her,

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:22.650
coming forward to say exactly
about freedom of speech.

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:25.380
Then why has she been treated in this way?

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:27.000
Why has there been this?

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:30.390
And, I wonder if things
are changing or turning.

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:31.980
I don't know what your take is.

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:34.247
I dunno, have you ever
spoken to her about this?

00:13:34.247 --> 00:13:35.080
No, no, no.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:36.388
I haven't spoken to her.

00:13:36.388 --> 00:13:39.690
But, so, when I started reading

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:40.920
about how she was being attacked,

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:42.990
I then went and read what she'd written,

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:45.060
which I thought was perfectly reasonable.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:47.580
And, I did not see why
she needed to be attacked.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:50.490
And, I think that she's
been treated abominably.

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:51.870
And, I think a lot of that treatment

00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:53.120
is because she's a woman.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:57.540
I think that a man who aired his views,

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:01.890
reasonable views, would
not be treated in that way.

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:03.900
And, there is also something
I think very dangerous

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:06.483
about refusing to engage
with what she said,

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:11.580
but, instead making it,
instead of hurling insults.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.680
But I read, I remember
reading what she'd written

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:15.751
and underlining bits, because...

00:14:15.751 --> 00:14:16.920
This is the essay on sex and gender.

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:17.753
Yeah.

00:14:17.753 --> 00:14:18.586
... underlining bits of it,

00:14:18.586 --> 00:14:20.370
because, some young
people in Lagos had said,

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:22.440
Oh, she's terrible, she's transphobic,

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:24.600
she wants to kill trans people.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:27.870
And, so, I said to them,
where does it say that here?

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:29.280
And, I don't even mean a literal sense,

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:30.870
because, there are ways
that you can read something

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:32.263
to mean something else,

00:14:32.263 --> 00:14:34.983
but, none of them could
point it out to me.

00:14:34.983 --> 00:14:36.390
And, I suppose this goes

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:39.180
to one of the essays you wrote, called,

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:40.013
It's Obscene.

00:14:40.013 --> 00:14:41.130
You talk about cancel culture

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:42.243
and lots of other things in there.

00:14:42.243 --> 00:14:45.180
But, you do express this
concern, this particular quote,

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.950
you say, "we have a
generation of young people

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:50.670
on social media so terrified
of having the wrong opinions

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:52.590
that they've robbed
themselves of the opportunity

00:14:52.590 --> 00:14:55.173
to think, and to learn, and to grow."

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:58.920
You talk to young
people, you lecture them.

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:01.320
I saw recently, you told them
to get offline for three weeks

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:02.343
and read some books.

00:15:03.694 --> 00:15:04.590
But, it's a serious issue.

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:06.090
And, I know you're
probably thinking about it

00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:10.020
as well as a parent, but
what can we do about this?

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:12.386
Whew, we can try being adults.

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:17.550
I sometimes feel as
though we have abdicated

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:19.650
our responsibility as the grownups,

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:23.160
because, I know what it
was like to be young.

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:24.560
I thought I knew everything.

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:29.670
Now, I look back, I'm
like, I knew nothing.

00:15:29.670 --> 00:15:34.080
I was wrong in many of my
sort of fierce positions.

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:36.180
And, I was fortunate
that when I was young,

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:37.770
there were adults who
were willing to tell me,

00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:39.780
you're actually really
not right about that.

00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:41.670
Here's what you should
think about differently.

00:15:41.670 --> 00:15:42.963
That's not happening now.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:47.160
We're also not saying to young people,

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:48.573
you cannot, I mean,

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.920
so, I remember talking to students

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:54.120
at a university in the US,

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:55.320
and it really made me sad,

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:57.870
because, well, first of all,

00:15:57.870 --> 00:16:01.050
we meet and I'm asking them questions,

00:16:01.050 --> 00:16:05.190
and it felt as though they
were repeating party lines

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:07.590
in a way, about just general issues.

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:09.420
I think it's tough for them.

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:10.470
And, I just, you know,

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:12.873
so, I did a little preaching, and I said,

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:16.770
can we all agree on compassion?

00:16:16.770 --> 00:16:20.070
Can we all agree that
not everyone means harm?

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:23.250
Can we all agree that people
can learn and people change?

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:25.110
You know, just sort of basic things

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:28.713
that we seem to have forgotten, I think.

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:32.107
There are lots of things I don't know.

00:16:32.107 --> 00:16:34.620
I mean, there're things
that I don't understand.

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:36.480
And, again, not to go back to the famous

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:38.580
A trans woman is a trans woman.

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:39.960
I thought I was saying something obvious.

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:42.030
I did not mean to offend anyone.

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:45.060
And, later I thought, wait,

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:47.010
what have I missed,
things I don't understand.

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:48.810
So, I went back and I
started reading things,

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:51.570
because, I just thought I
really didn't understand

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:55.140
what the noise was about.

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:57.210
And, so, I think about
young people and I think,

00:16:57.210 --> 00:16:59.460
I'm sure there are many
people in my position,

00:16:59.460 --> 00:17:02.550
which is to say, do
not know the orthodoxy.

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:03.450
Right.

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:05.820
And, who should be given
a chance to know it

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:08.820
and then choose whether
to accept it or reject it?

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:10.950
I don't think that they're
giving that chance.

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:13.530
Yeah, well, and also, they don't feel

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:14.850
they have the space necessarily,

00:17:14.850 --> 00:17:17.160
to have those conversations.

00:17:17.160 --> 00:17:19.080
A lot of people want to hear, I suppose,

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:21.690
where your feminism is up to.

00:17:21.690 --> 00:17:24.870
And, I wonder, because,
feminism in that time

00:17:24.870 --> 00:17:26.070
is less of a dirty word.

00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:31.070
Certainly in the Western world,
it's being sold as a brand.

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:34.290
It's by fashion, by the media.

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:37.170
But, I wonder if it has
achieved what it's meant

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:38.403
to have achieved, yet?

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:40.110
No, of course not.

00:17:40.110 --> 00:17:43.080
It's not going to achieve
it in the next 100 years.

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:44.880
It took a long time for us to get here.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:49.880
We've had, really, human
history, the sort of,

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:53.420
the length of time that
we've been here on earth,

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:57.393
we've had men be in charge.

00:17:58.320 --> 00:17:59.910
And, if we're going to achieve equality,

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:00.900
it's going to take a long time,

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:02.640
so, that it doesn't,

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:03.473
I think for me,

00:18:03.473 --> 00:18:05.940
maybe, the question I
would be interested in is,

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:07.650
are we taking a few steps forward?

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:10.500
But, at the same time
we're seeing marching,

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:11.940
we're seeing protests,

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:16.940
and since the death of
Mahsa Amini in Iran,

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:18.810
who was held by Iran's morality police

00:18:18.810 --> 00:18:20.730
for not covering her hair properly.

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:23.130
She died on the 16th of September.

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:26.610
Those protests are carrying
on, led by women in Iran.

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:28.140
And, also, in Afghanistan,

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:30.000
women trying to go back to university,

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.560
or their jobs, since
the Taliban took over.

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:32.583
It's very striking.

00:18:33.903 --> 00:18:36.090
There's a footage going of a few women

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:37.350
being whipped by Taliban...

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:38.210
Oh, I saw that this morning.

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:40.350
...outside of the University.

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:42.930
But, chanting, Women, Life, Freedom,

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:45.990
which is the same words
chosen by women in Iran.

00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:49.500
And, while it's awful what's being denied,

00:18:49.500 --> 00:18:52.410
there's also something
in what's being said.

00:18:52.410 --> 00:18:55.200
Oh, I find it enormously inspiring.

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:59.730
I think that they are just absolute stars.

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:02.100
Particularly the women
in Iran and Afghanistan.

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:06.720
Because, you think about what
they're standing up against.

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:09.060
And, I felt that that the whipping video

00:19:09.060 --> 00:19:13.501
is such a dramatic example of,

00:19:13.501 --> 00:19:16.110
of what their lives are like.

00:19:16.110 --> 00:19:17.610
But, they're still pushing back.

00:19:17.610 --> 00:19:19.380
I just find it so inspiring.

00:19:19.380 --> 00:19:21.390
And, the women in Iran when
they're cutting their hair

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:22.920
and they're throwing
the thing in the fire.

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:26.680
I actually was weeping
watching one of the videos.

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:28.453
It was incredibly emotional to say.

00:19:28.453 --> 00:19:30.480
And, also, it's happening, now.

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:32.970
You know, to get your
head around that as well.

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:34.890
I always wondered if
you found it frustrating

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:37.380
that some came to your
work through Beyonce,

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:39.873
as opposed to finding you themselves?

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:46.560
No, I'll tell you one of the reasons

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:50.123
that I was happy to
do, happy to say yes...

00:19:50.123 --> 00:19:51.270
This is because she,

00:19:51.270 --> 00:19:53.085
just to say if people dunno,

00:19:53.085 --> 00:19:54.240
she took some of your words,

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:55.950
sampled some of your words for a song.

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:57.038
Yes.

00:19:57.038 --> 00:19:57.871
About feminism.

00:19:57.871 --> 00:19:59.469
It's because I thought

00:19:59.469 --> 00:20:00.750
that she has such a huge following

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:01.583
that there are going to be people

00:20:01.583 --> 00:20:03.810
who never heard the word feminism,

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:06.720
never thought about it,
who will because of this.

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:08.580
And, for me, that's a wonderful thing.

00:20:08.580 --> 00:20:10.620
But, to have people come
to my readings and say,

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:13.530
Oh, I never heard about you,
then I heard you on Beyonce,

00:20:13.530 --> 00:20:15.660
and then they don't want to
ask me anything about my books.

00:20:15.660 --> 00:20:18.060
they want to ask me, so Beyonce,

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:19.440
that doesn't interest me very much.

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:20.813
It doesn't work for you.

00:20:23.010 --> 00:20:24.750
That's not the way this is gonna work.

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:26.580
No, but, I think also

00:20:26.580 --> 00:20:28.050
there's a part of me that just,

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:29.580
I feel this way about films as well.

00:20:29.580 --> 00:20:30.510
When people say,

00:20:30.510 --> 00:20:33.600
Oh, it's fantastic, your
book was made into a film.

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:37.890
And, I'm thinking it's nice,
but, I wrote the bloody book.

00:20:37.890 --> 00:20:39.180
I want to talk about books.

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:40.500
I want books to be cool.

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:44.370
I want be excited about books,
you know, and we're just not.

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:47.700
And, it just, I just feel
deeply, deeply annoyed by that.

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:48.810
But, it's, you know,

00:20:48.810 --> 00:20:50.790
whether we have the wrong heroes and

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:53.070
how we look at the world
and implement things for us.

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:54.090
I do, I agree.

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:57.180
And, also what the responsibility
that those heroes have,

00:20:57.180 --> 00:20:59.100
and I'm wary of talking about this

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:00.600
because, I do not like people telling me,

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:03.390
you're a role model therefore
you should not do this.

00:21:03.390 --> 00:21:04.320
But, I think for example,

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:07.560
that Beyonce making the decision,

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:09.480
and people can think what they will

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:11.280
about talking about feminism,

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:13.140
I think it's a really good thing.

00:21:13.140 --> 00:21:15.480
Because, and I know for a fact

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:17.160
that there are many young people, who,

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:20.100
because of that, suddenly,
I know relatives,

00:21:20.100 --> 00:21:24.330
young nieces, who, auntie
talks about feminism.

00:21:24.330 --> 00:21:26.820
Auntie writes books, Beyonce
talks about feminism.

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:28.020
They're like, I'm a feminist.

00:21:28.020 --> 00:21:29.954
And, now, they're making what they think

00:21:29.954 --> 00:21:30.787
are feminist arguments.

00:21:30.787 --> 00:21:32.790
And, I think it's fantastic because,

00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:34.290
conversations are happening when...

00:21:34.290 --> 00:21:35.400
It's good to hear that Beyonce

00:21:35.400 --> 00:21:36.978
helped you get closer to your family.

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:43.023
No, among a certain sort of cohort,

00:21:44.023 --> 00:21:45.600
the family, they suddenly thought,

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:47.110
Hmm, auntie's not too bad.

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:47.943
She's okay.

00:21:47.943 --> 00:21:49.470
She's, you know, we can, Yeah.

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:51.060
She's tolerable now.

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:52.260
What are you working on now?

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:53.220
I cannot tell you.

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:54.053
You can't tell me,

00:21:54.053 --> 00:21:55.632
you don't like saying it, do you?

00:21:55.632 --> 00:21:56.465
Until it's done, okay.

00:21:56.465 --> 00:21:58.486
But, writing, I imagine.

00:21:58.486 --> 00:22:00.810
Or? Okay.

00:22:00.810 --> 00:22:04.350
The creativity though, has it
come back since your daughter?

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:05.183
Is it coming?

00:22:05.183 --> 00:22:06.330
It's slowly coming back.

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:08.065
But, I think again,

00:22:08.065 --> 00:22:09.270
that's amazing for women in particular

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:10.890
to be able to hear you say that.

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:12.750
Because, again, it's not something

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:15.450
that's talked about, a long after effect.

00:22:15.450 --> 00:22:16.320
Yes, yes.

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:18.180
I think it's really
important to talk about.

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:21.390
And, also to talk about
it, knowing that we love

00:22:21.390 --> 00:22:25.020
that we had the gift of
motherhood and babies,

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:26.270
and we adored them.

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:27.103
Such a gift if you want it.

00:22:27.103 --> 00:22:29.180
It really is, it really is.

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:32.430
But, you know, we should
talk about the other things

00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:33.600
because, nobody told me about the,

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:36.813
I had horrible joint pains, my fingers,

00:22:39.030 --> 00:22:42.300
and nobody told me about the constipation.

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:45.480
And, I just feel like we need
to talk about these things.

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:47.430
And, you laugh at, when
I say constipation,

00:22:47.430 --> 00:22:49.950
which apparently, is the
puzzling response I get

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:51.469
from other people as well.

00:22:51.469 --> 00:22:56.190
No, we should talk about,
I personally was shocked

00:22:56.190 --> 00:22:57.210
and I was like, what's happening?

00:22:57.210 --> 00:22:59.910
And, my husband is a
doctor, and he was sort of,

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:00.743
Oh, you didn't know.

00:23:00.743 --> 00:23:02.340
And, I wanted to punch his face.

00:23:02.340 --> 00:23:04.373
I was like, Of course I didn't know.

00:23:04.373 --> 00:23:05.820
Well, there's two types
of constipation here.

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:07.290
There's the literal and the creative.

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:08.123
Of course.

00:23:08.123 --> 00:23:09.083
So, we've covered both.

00:23:10.020 --> 00:23:12.418
I like that actually, fantastic.

00:23:12.418 --> 00:23:14.610
Chimamanda, thank you very
much for talking to me

00:23:14.610 --> 00:23:16.710
and to all of us today.

00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:19.410
It's been a pleasure
and memorable as always.

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:20.580
Thank you Emma.

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:23.100
And, thank you so much for being with us.

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:25.825
